21:00.480 --> 21:03.480
Say the cell is stressed or the gene sequence is difficult to read.

21:03.620 --> 21:10.080
When TE is weak, the polymerase engine keeps moving fast, but the ribosome train car lags behind.

21:10.640 --> 21:11.560
The coupling stretches.

21:12.120 --> 21:16.900
This exposes a length of bare, untethered transcript called the spacer RNA.

21:16.900 --> 21:17.880
Uh-oh.

21:17.880 --> 21:19.720
And nature abhors a vacuum.

21:20.440 --> 21:27.420
When this spacer is exposed, a specific protein called the rho-helicase spots the gap and latches onto the bare RNA.

21:27.660 --> 21:29.000
What does the rho-helicase do?

21:29.440 --> 21:30.820
It acts as a termination factor.

21:31.300 --> 21:35.360
It climbs up the RNA and forces the transcript to release from the polymerase prematurely.

21:35.700 --> 21:38.600
It physically yanks the work order off the assembly line.

21:38.600 --> 21:39.040
Wow.

21:39.240 --> 21:41.060
And once it releases from the center of the room?

21:41.060 --> 21:42.900
It diffuses into the cytoplasm.

21:43.060 --> 21:43.200
Right.

21:43.320 --> 21:47.160
It drifts freely through the fluid until it hits the inner membrane where RNA's E is waiting.

21:47.640 --> 21:50.160
The shredder catches it, and it is instantly destroyed.

21:50.160 --> 21:52.440
The microscopic trajectory collapses.

21:52.440 --> 21:54.440
That is an incredible mechanism.

21:54.440 --> 22:01.580
But, I mean, how do we know this spatial separation is actually the key, and not just a coincidence of how E. coli is built?

22:01.980 --> 22:05.720
The paper compares this to another species to prove the point, right?

22:05.720 --> 22:10.520
Yes, and this comparative biology is the smoking gun for the geometric theory.

22:10.900 --> 22:13.560
They look at a different bacterium called Colobacter chryscentis.

22:13.740 --> 22:15.500
Okay, how is Colobacter different?

22:15.840 --> 22:19.480
In Colobacter, the RNA's E enzyme is not anchored to the membrane.

22:20.080 --> 22:21.280
It evolved differently.

22:21.740 --> 22:23.400
It floats freely in the cytoplasm.

22:23.440 --> 22:25.100
So the shredder is unbolted.

22:25.160 --> 22:26.420
It's just roaming the room.

22:26.520 --> 22:26.900
Exactly.

22:27.120 --> 22:30.760
Because of this, the spatial admissibility geometry completely collapses.

22:30.760 --> 22:39.680
As a result, the rate of degradation of transcripts, while they are still being actively made, is roughly 10 times higher in Colobacter than in E. coli.

22:39.780 --> 22:40.420
10 times.

22:40.620 --> 22:40.800
Yeah.

22:41.220 --> 22:44.380
The system is vastly more fragile because the spatial separation is gone.

22:44.540 --> 22:45.980
But Colobacter isn't extinct.

22:46.120 --> 22:47.100
It still survives.

22:47.540 --> 22:53.720
Right, and what's fascinating here is that both species achieve the same final functional invariant.

22:54.040 --> 22:57.860
They both successfully regulate their genes and survive as organisms.

22:57.860 --> 23:02.540
But they use completely different admissibility geometries to do it.

23:03.120 --> 23:10.320
Colobacter has to compensate with other mechanisms, like incredibly fast transcription rates, just to outrun the roaming shredders.

23:10.440 --> 23:12.300
There is a beautiful symmetry here.

23:12.500 --> 23:13.680
Think about what we just learned.

23:14.000 --> 23:19.520
In the cryptography example, we hid the data by crowding the room with random noise that was mixing.

23:19.680 --> 23:19.840
Right.

23:19.840 --> 23:25.680
In the bacteria, we protect the data by putting it in a separate room entirely, that is, spatial separation.

23:26.000 --> 23:29.840
But structurally, mathematically, they're doing the exact same thing.

23:29.960 --> 23:31.620
They are formally isomorphic.

23:32.100 --> 23:36.140
Both mechanisms prevent an observer, whether that's a malicious hacker trying to steal medical data,

23:36.440 --> 23:43.140
or a hungry shredder enzyme trying to eat RNA from extending their local information to a global reconstruction.

23:43.140 --> 23:43.700
Meh.

23:44.360 --> 23:50.140
One does it by making the fiber too crowded to decode, and the other does it by making the physical path obstructed.

23:50.860 --> 23:54.480
But mathematically, it is the exact same topological obstruction.

23:54.720 --> 23:55.460
It really is.

23:55.660 --> 24:01.180
Okay, so we've seen this play out in abstract computer code and in literal bacterial guts.

24:01.620 --> 24:05.020
Now we really need to look at the unified physics that explains both.

24:05.360 --> 24:10.500
Because if these two entirely different domains follow the same rules, there has to be a master equation.

24:10.920 --> 24:11.340
There is.

24:11.340 --> 24:13.900
The paper introduces the RSVP framework.

24:14.460 --> 24:19.100
This is the math that predicts exactly when these systems succeed and when they face total collapse.

24:19.440 --> 24:21.220
So break down RSVP for me.

24:21.820 --> 24:24.900
RSVP stands for Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum.

24:25.260 --> 24:31.660
It is a formal differential geometry framework that posits that any sufficiently complex dynamical system,

24:31.820 --> 24:37.000
whether it's a server farm, a cell, or society, can be mapped using three interacting fields.

24:37.080 --> 24:38.460
Okay, let's take these one by one.

24:38.540 --> 24:39.420
What is the first field?

24:39.420 --> 24:41.740
The first is phi, the admissibility density.

24:42.180 --> 24:47.760
This is a scalar field, which basically means it assigns a single magnitude or number to every point in space.

24:47.980 --> 24:50.840
It represents how safe or stable a configuration is.

24:51.100 --> 24:53.940
I need a functional metaphor for this, not just a mathematical definition.

24:54.180 --> 24:56.400
How should I picture these three fields interacting?

24:56.760 --> 24:57.000
Sure.

24:57.000 --> 25:02.140
Imagine phi, the admissibility density, as the physical depth of a massive canyon.

25:02.660 --> 25:07.980
The deeper the canyon, the higher the phi value, and the safer you are from spilling over the edge.

25:08.100 --> 25:10.420
Okay, deep canyon equals high safety.

25:10.980 --> 25:12.100
What is the second field?

25:12.100 --> 25:14.600
The second is V, the trajectory flow.

25:14.800 --> 25:18.680
This is a vector field, meaning it has both magnitude and direction.

25:19.160 --> 25:22.060
It shows the speed and direction that things are moving through the system.

25:22.060 --> 25:26.740
So if phi is the canyon, V is the physical river of water rushing through it.

25:26.740 --> 25:27.180
Exactly.

25:27.480 --> 25:33.740
The flow of data in a network, the physical movement of the mRNA transcript away from the nucleoid, the progression of time.

25:34.020 --> 25:36.760
And the third field is S, the entropy field.

25:36.840 --> 25:37.960
And S measures what?

25:37.960 --> 25:43.760
This measures the local degree of randomness, noise, or microscopic indistinguishability in the system.

25:44.220 --> 25:50.240
So in our river canyon, S is the turbulence, the splashing, the whitewater rapids, the chaotic swirling of the water.

25:50.500 --> 25:50.560
Yes.

25:50.680 --> 25:56.020
Okay, so we have the canyon depth, which is phi, the river flow, which is V, and the water turbulence, S.

25:56.660 --> 25:58.700
How do they define success or failure?

25:59.220 --> 26:05.980
Together, they define what the author calls the admissibility bubble, or B, and its critical boundary, partial B.

26:05.980 --> 26:08.260
So what happens inside the bubble?

26:08.540 --> 26:12.240
Inside the bubble, deep inside the canyon, you have high admissibility density.

26:12.980 --> 26:17.160
Small perturbations, little errors, minor random noise, none of it matters.

26:17.940 --> 26:19.000
The projection is stable.

26:19.320 --> 26:20.720
The system absorbs the chaos.

26:21.380 --> 26:28.220
You can have incredibly turbulent whitewater rapids, but because the canyon is so deep, the water still flows predictably to the ocean.

26:28.580 --> 26:29.980
But what happens when you hit the boundary?

26:30.400 --> 26:34.920
If your trajectory crosses that boundary, you experience catastrophic projection collapse.

26:34.920 --> 26:38.340
The system permanently loses its functional invariant.

26:38.920 --> 26:39.860
The map is destroyed.

26:40.460 --> 26:41.940
So how does entropy cause that?

26:42.080 --> 26:45.920
Because earlier in cryptography, we said entropy was our friend.

26:46.220 --> 26:47.580
The noise protected the data.

26:48.000 --> 26:50.380
But now we are saying noise can cause collapse.

26:50.740 --> 26:52.960
It depends entirely on the direction of the entropy flow.

26:53.240 --> 26:56.480
This is the absolute crux of the RSVP mathematics.

26:56.920 --> 27:00.800
We have to distinguish between tangential entropy and transverse entropy.

27:01.020 --> 27:01.640
Break that down.

27:01.700 --> 27:02.860
What is tangential entropy?

27:02.860 --> 27:07.140
Pangential entropy flows along the surface of the manifold without pointing out of it.

27:07.780 --> 27:14.120
In our canyon metaphor, this is turbulence that pushes the water forward or backward or sideways along the riverbed,

27:14.540 --> 27:17.380
splashing against the high rock walls, but never pushing it.

27:17.720 --> 27:19.520
Okay, so the water stays in the canyon.

27:19.940 --> 27:20.240
Yes.

27:20.660 --> 27:23.680
That is the random noise in the ARE crypto shuffle.

27:24.200 --> 27:29.240
It just spreads the data out along the safe surface, making the fibers larger and the privacy stronger.

27:29.240 --> 27:34.140
The turbulence actually hides the individual water droplets, which is exactly what we want.

27:34.260 --> 27:38.460
It's like adding static to a radio broadcast that is on the exact same frequency, right?

27:38.640 --> 27:43.560
It might be noisy, but the underlying song is still perfectly contained within that station's bandwidth.

27:43.940 --> 27:44.460
Precisely.

27:45.100 --> 27:47.060
But transverse entropy is entirely different.

27:47.060 --> 27:53.780
If the entropy flow acquires a transverse component, it means the randomness pushes perpendicular to the safe surface.

27:54.060 --> 27:56.460
It drives the system straight into the bubble boundary.

27:56.700 --> 28:02.320
So in the canyon, transverse entropy is a geyser pushing the water violently upward over the rim of the canyon,

28:02.720 --> 28:04.800
spilling it out into the desert where it evaporates.

28:05.180 --> 28:06.360
The river is destroyed.

28:06.640 --> 28:06.900
Yes.

28:07.580 --> 28:12.320
And that is exactly what happens in the E. coli bacterium when the translation efficiency is weak.

28:12.380 --> 28:14.840
The gap between the polymerase and the ribosome.

28:14.840 --> 28:16.900
Right. It creates a transverse divergence.

28:17.540 --> 28:22.820
The entropy flows into that exposed spacer RNA, allowing the row helicase to bind.

28:23.380 --> 28:27.000
The transcript is pulled transversely out of the safe nucleoid canyon.

28:27.640 --> 28:31.920
It hits the boundary of the membrane, and the function is completely destroyed by the shredder.

28:32.440 --> 28:36.920
So failure in these systems isn't about a mechanical gear snapping in half.

28:36.980 --> 28:38.560
It isn't a piece of hardware breaking.

28:38.560 --> 28:45.180
It is about a system accidentally wandering out of its safe geometric zone due to transverse noise.

28:45.580 --> 28:48.160
Catastrophic projection collapse is a mapping failure.

28:49.000 --> 28:56.260
It's when the system's projection accidentally maps two functionally distinct microscopic states to the exact same macroscopic output,

28:56.520 --> 28:59.660
or maps a necessary state to an invalid output.

28:59.660 --> 29:03.620
The projection stops preserving the structure and starts destroying the meaning.

29:03.760 --> 29:05.140
The water leaves the canyon.

29:05.400 --> 29:11.700
It's like a typo that completely changes the meaning of a sentence, say, changing I love you to I leave you,

29:11.820 --> 29:14.980
instead of just being a spelling error you can comfortably read past.

29:16.060 --> 29:21.840
Which actually makes me wonder, what happens when you have millions of these little admissibility bubbles trying to work together?

29:22.200 --> 29:23.440
Ah, scaling up.

29:23.440 --> 29:23.840
Yeah.

29:24.180 --> 29:28.060
We've looked at single bacterial cells and isolated crypto equations.

29:28.340 --> 29:29.100
Let's scale up.

29:29.920 --> 29:34.760
How do complex, multicellular things grow without a central brain coordinating all the bubbles?

29:35.400 --> 29:42.720
The paper uses a stunning 2026 single nucleus atlas of Arabidopsis seed development to answer exactly this question.

29:42.760 --> 29:43.720
Okay, a plant seed.

29:43.900 --> 29:44.100
Yeah.

29:44.100 --> 29:49.960
When a plant seed is developing, it has to coordinate three entirely distinct main tissue types.

29:50.400 --> 29:52.140
There's the embryo, which will become the plant.

29:52.140 --> 29:56.900
The endosperm, which provides the nutrients, and the seed coat, which provides the protection.

29:57.360 --> 30:01.640
What is absolutely critical here is that these three tissues are simplastically isolated.

30:01.840 --> 30:03.060
Wait, paint a picture of that.

30:03.360 --> 30:06.900
If they're all growing together inside a tiny seed, how are they isolated?

30:07.020 --> 30:08.400
Are there brick walls between them?

30:08.700 --> 30:09.600
Essentially, yes.

30:10.280 --> 30:18.560
In plants, cells usually share resources and chemical messages through tiny pores in their cell walls called plasmotsmata.

30:19.120 --> 30:21.680
It allows the cytoplasm to flow freely between them.

30:21.680 --> 30:22.480
Like little tunnels.

30:22.900 --> 30:23.140
Right.

30:23.500 --> 30:28.020
But between the embryo, the endosperm, and the seed coat, there are no plasmotsmata.

30:28.700 --> 30:30.360
The cell walls are solid.

30:30.520 --> 30:31.820
They do not share cytoplasm.

30:32.020 --> 30:34.780
They cannot share their full internal molecular state.

30:34.900 --> 30:36.200
So there is no central blueprint.

30:36.540 --> 30:42.860
There is no master DNA computer sitting in the embryo sending a complete CAD file of how to build a plant to every other cell.

30:42.860 --> 30:48.440
If they are physically walled off, how do they coordinate their growth perfectly every single time?

30:48.980 --> 30:52.280
The paper explains that they use short-secreted peptides, or SSPs.

30:52.540 --> 30:58.740
Because they are simplastically isolated, they cannot share their massive internal trajectory space X.

30:59.280 --> 31:04.520
So instead, they pass these tiny, low-dimensional signals across the rigid borders between the tissues.

31:04.640 --> 31:06.080
They are passing notes under the door.

31:06.080 --> 31:10.180
Yes, but the notes do not carry the full microscopic state of the sending tissue.

31:10.340 --> 31:12.440
They act as low-dimensional coordination operators.

31:12.680 --> 31:14.320
They just carry a constraint update.

31:14.460 --> 31:15.720
Like, what does the note say?

31:16.040 --> 31:19.800
An SSP basically crosses the border and says,

31:19.800 --> 31:33.720
It's like a factory where the workers in room A can't see what the workers in room B are doing, and they can't even talk to them.

31:33.980 --> 31:37.160
But there is a single red or green light above the door.

31:37.540 --> 31:37.780
Right.

31:37.780 --> 31:41.140
When room A finishes their part, they flip the light to green.

31:41.700 --> 31:44.560
Room B sees the green light and starts their machinery.

31:44.820 --> 31:47.300
They don't need to know how room A did their job.

31:47.440 --> 31:50.360
They just need the binary constraint update.

31:50.700 --> 31:54.360
And the paper gets very specific about the physical geography of this light switch.

31:54.500 --> 31:57.960
It focuses on a region called the chalizal endosperm.

31:58.020 --> 32:00.620
What makes the chalizal endosperm special?

32:01.060 --> 32:03.260
It acts as a developmental interface manifold.

32:03.260 --> 32:07.800
It has a very specific apical basal polarity, meaning it has a distinct top and bottom.

32:08.380 --> 32:10.740
It sits physically at the crossroads of the seed.

32:11.180 --> 32:15.980
It is a geometric region where the multiple admissibility flows from the walled-off tissues converge,

32:16.520 --> 32:20.040
interact based on those tiny peptide constraints, and then separate again.

32:20.240 --> 32:26.860
The paper also talks about a hormone, brassinosteroid, acting like a gravitational field in this space.

32:27.060 --> 32:30.080
That is a crucial part of the RSVP mapping.

32:30.080 --> 32:36.280
The brassinosteroid hormones doesn't act like a traditional message that a cell reads and interprets.

32:36.860 --> 32:41.560
Instead, it establishes a non-uniform admissibility field across the seed.

32:41.940 --> 32:43.520
It changes the field itself.

32:43.600 --> 32:43.820
Yes.

32:44.280 --> 32:47.620
It physically changes the canyon depth across the space.

32:48.300 --> 32:50.720
The developing cells don't need to actually read the hormone.

32:50.900 --> 32:54.580
They just passively follow the physical gradient of the field.

32:54.760 --> 32:57.200
Like a marble rolling down a warped trampoline.

32:57.320 --> 32:57.760
Exactly.

32:57.760 --> 33:00.680
The marble doesn't know it's being told to go to the center.

33:00.980 --> 33:03.280
The geometry of the fabric just forces it there.

33:03.620 --> 33:07.040
It perfectly matches the flocking behavior analogy the text brings up.

33:07.140 --> 33:07.700
Right, the birds.

33:07.900 --> 33:10.420
Yeah, think about a massive flock of starlings in the sky.

33:10.820 --> 33:13.840
There is no CEO bird at the front telling the flock where to turn.

33:14.220 --> 33:19.020
Each bird is just looking at its immediate neighbors and adjusting its flight path to avoid hitting them.

33:19.720 --> 33:23.620
Each seed tissue just responds to local constraint updates at the borders.

33:23.620 --> 33:30.720
And the global fully formed plant is the invariant that survives the chaotic distributed communication.

33:30.860 --> 33:31.760
It's beautiful.

33:31.900 --> 33:36.520
And this distributed chaotic border communication raises a fascinating point about evolution.

33:37.260 --> 33:43.660
The paper notes that the most rapid evolutionary changes in the seed happen exactly at these tissue interfaces.

33:43.660 --> 33:44.300
Why there?

33:44.520 --> 33:45.740
Because of genetic conflict.

33:46.520 --> 33:55.760
The maternal tissue, the seed coat, and the paternal or hybrid tissue, the endosperm, often have competing interests regarding how many resources to consume.

33:55.940 --> 34:00.100
So they are constantly in an evolutionary arms race at the border.

34:00.320 --> 34:00.540
Yep.

34:00.760 --> 34:05.660
The molecular machinery passing the notes under the door is constantly mutating, trying to gain an advantage.

34:05.940 --> 34:10.740
The microscopic trajectory is incredibly chaotic and volatile over evolutionary time.

34:10.740 --> 34:11.240
Exactly.

34:11.680 --> 34:13.540
But the global seed invariant survives.

34:13.740 --> 34:14.580
The plant still grows.

34:15.140 --> 34:17.620
Function survives the microstatic evolutionary collapse.

34:18.160 --> 34:22.700
The seed uses the chaos at the interface to constantly refine its admissibility projection.

34:23.060 --> 34:25.840
Okay, this is blowing my mind, but I want to bring it even closer to home.

34:26.260 --> 34:35.900
Because the author argues that the exact same math governing a growing plant seed or a bacterial shredder governs how you and I are communicating right now.

34:36.360 --> 34:39.720
Yes, the literal physics of human perception.

34:39.720 --> 34:43.180
Let's apply the RSVP framework to how we read and hear.

34:43.440 --> 34:46.680
This is the cognitive application of the theory, and it is brilliant.

34:47.180 --> 34:48.380
Let's start with visual perception.

34:49.180 --> 34:49.660
Typography.

34:50.380 --> 34:52.460
Think of a glyph, like the capital letter A.

34:52.600 --> 34:55.880
Okay, picturing an A, two slanted lines, and a crossbar.

34:56.040 --> 34:56.940
What makes it an A?

34:57.400 --> 34:59.060
It is not a specific shape.

34:59.560 --> 35:05.280
It is not a specific bitmap of pixels on a screen or a specific set of pen strokes on paper.

35:05.280 --> 35:07.440
A glyph is an equivalence class.

35:07.900 --> 35:10.480
In our math, it is a projection manifold M-semantic.

35:10.600 --> 35:11.260
M-semantic.

35:11.420 --> 35:20.780
When you read a word, your brain takes the precise microscopic stroke, details the X-stroke trajectory, and completely destroys them to extract the semantic meaning.

35:20.900 --> 35:21.820
That makes total sense.

35:21.960 --> 35:29.020
I don't care if the A is written in standard Arial font or messy cursive handwriting, or spray-painted on a brick wall with dripping paint.

35:29.020 --> 35:34.500
My brain throws away the paint, drips, throws away the cursive loops, and just keeps the A.

35:34.560 --> 35:35.040
Exactly.

35:35.260 --> 35:37.660
Your brain is performing an admissible projection.

35:38.140 --> 35:46.660
It is consuming the chaotic territory of the ink to preserve the map of the alphabet, and we can map this directly to the RSVP fields.

35:46.820 --> 35:47.260
How so?

35:47.480 --> 35:50.480
Think of typography styles as dynamic flows in the field.

35:51.120 --> 35:54.020
If a designer makes a font bold, what are they doing geometrically?

35:54.020 --> 35:57.120
They are initiating a low entropy flow.

35:57.660 --> 36:02.100
By increasing the stroke mass, they are increasing the admissibility density phi.

36:02.300 --> 36:03.620
They are making the canyon deeper.

36:04.000 --> 36:04.420
Exactly.

36:04.860 --> 36:10.260
The letter becomes more robust against visual noise, which is why highway signs use thick, bold fonts.

36:10.400 --> 36:11.900
Okay, what about an italic font?

36:12.140 --> 36:13.620
Italic is a sheer flow.

36:14.280 --> 36:20.760
It displaces the pixels horizontally across the field, but crucially, it preserves the topological skeleton of the letter.

36:20.760 --> 36:25.160
It is an admissible flow because it stays deep inside the admissibility bubble.

36:25.500 --> 36:26.740
The structure isn't broken.

36:27.060 --> 36:28.500
But what about crazy fonts?

36:28.680 --> 36:34.040
Think about heavy metal band logos that look like tangles of thorns or chaotic overlapping street graffiti.

36:34.480 --> 36:35.700
How can I still read that?

36:35.960 --> 36:38.200
That is what the paper calls frenetic typography.

36:39.060 --> 36:43.040
A heavy metal logo injects massive amounts of visual entropy into the field.

36:43.580 --> 36:47.680
There are jagged edges, varying line weights, visual noise everywhere.

36:47.840 --> 36:49.100
So why doesn't it collapse?

36:49.100 --> 36:50.720
Because of the direction.

36:51.060 --> 36:52.500
This is the key to why it works.

36:53.720 --> 36:57.260
The entropy is injected tangentially to the semantic manifold.

36:57.660 --> 36:59.100
Tangential, right, like the radiostatic.

36:59.780 --> 37:00.220
Exactly.

37:00.440 --> 37:07.340
It dances around the edges of the letter's structural skeleton, adding noise along the surface, without ever breaking the core topology.

37:08.080 --> 37:12.960
Because the entropy is tangential, it remains readable, even though it's incredibly noisy.

37:12.960 --> 37:14.680
Just like the cryptography shuffle.

37:15.300 --> 37:19.180
The noise hides the exact pen strokes, but the meaning survives.

37:19.620 --> 37:23.020
And the paper contrasts that with hypnagogic typography.

37:23.240 --> 37:23.520
Yes.

37:24.060 --> 37:28.180
Hypnagogic typography is an oscillation right at the bubble boundary, partial B.

37:28.620 --> 37:35.660
It's a font or a visual design that slowly drifts in and out of legibility, intentionally weakening the local curvature constraints of the glyph.

37:35.740 --> 37:36.480
Drifting in and out.

37:36.480 --> 37:40.500
Yeah, your brain recognizes the letter, then the noise pushes it transversely.

37:40.600 --> 37:42.500
You lose the letter, and then you find it again.

37:42.500 --> 37:51.760
It creates a dreamlike, liminal state in the reader, because your brain is surfing the exact edge of catastrophic projection collapse.

37:52.120 --> 37:53.140
That is fascinating.

37:53.740 --> 37:55.580
But it's not just visual reading.

37:55.680 --> 38:00.480
The text applies this to the acoustic trajectory space, to human speech itself.

38:00.620 --> 38:08.200
If we think about temporal warping, the speed at which we talk, it acts as an admissible deformation of the acoustic manifold.

38:08.380 --> 38:08.820
Exactly.

38:08.820 --> 38:13.800
When a speaker accelerates their delivery talking incredibly fast, it acts as cognitive compression.

38:14.520 --> 38:19.500
They are increasing the local entropy at the acoustic field by packing more foams in to less time.

38:19.740 --> 38:20.980
But if they don't break the structure.

